</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Dynamic Knowledge Update</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We aim to fully leverage the knowledge acquired during the detection process to facilitate detection of similar news in the future. Additionally, dynamically updating the model’s knowledge allows it to stay informed about the development of events, thereby enhancing its capability to handle real-world scenarios. Therefore, for news confirmed as true in the detection results, we extract new knowledge from the question-and-answer process involving the news text and reasoning paths, which is used to update the knowledge graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The method for extracting triples here is the same as the approach used in Section  <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Knowledge Graph Construction ‣ 3 Methods ‣ A Dynamic Knowledge Update-Driven Model with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> for constructing the knowledge graph. Firstly, we extract key entities from the text to form a new entity set <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then input the entity set and the text into the LLM, instructing it to extract keywords that describe the relationships between relevant entities or directly generate new summary terms <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and output the corresponding triples <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we store the newly acquired triples <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\text{new}}=(E_{\text{new}},R_{\text{new}},S_{\text{new}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\text{new}}=(E_{\text{new}},R_{\text{new}},S_{\text{new}})</annotation></semantics></math> into the knowledge graph <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The updated <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> will be utilized in the subsequent news detection, and it will continuously be updated with new knowledge extracted from news that will be verified as true.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first introduce the datasets, baselines, and various settings used in the experiments (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Experiment Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ A Dynamic Knowledge Update-Driven Model with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). We validate the model’s performance on two real-world datasets (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Experiment Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ A Dynamic Knowledge Update-Driven Model with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Additionally, we design experiments to demonstrate the advantages of dynamic knowledge updating (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Evaluation on Knowledge Graph Update ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ A Dynamic Knowledge Update-Driven Model with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). Then, we conduct ablation experiments to verify the necessity of each component and the rationality of the parameter (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ A Dynamic Knowledge Update-Driven Model with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>). Finally, we present a case to showcase how dynamic knowledge updating aids fake news detection (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Case Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ A Dynamic Knowledge Update-Driven Model with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiment Setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
To validate the enhancement effect of the dynamically updated knowledge graph, we require datasets that comprise a series of news with similar content but differing keywords and labels. Additionally, we desire the datasets to include factual statements related to the news, which can be utilized to construct a news-specific knowledge graph. In summary, two real-world datasets are selected for experiments. Hover <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2020)</a></cite> is a dataset of multi-hop factual statements, containing 18,171 samples. It extracts facts from several Wikipedia articles related to factual statements and categorizes whether the statements are correct. Feverous <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aly et al. (2021)</a></cite>, with 26,928 samples, extracts structured evidence, unstructured evidence, or a combination of both from Wikipedia to verify statements. Since we focus solely on data in natural language form, we follow the setup of  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2023)</a></cite> and select only sentence evidence and corresponding statements as our samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare the proposed model with the following baseline models: (1) Traditional methods: RoBERTa-NLI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2020)</a></cite> utilizes a finely tuned version of RoBERTa-large across four natural language inference datasets and BERT-FC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soleimani et al. (2020)</a></cite> trains BERT to complete fact-checking tasks. GET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2022)</a></cite> implements graph-based semantic structure mining to learn long-distance semantic dependency relationships. (2) LLM-based methods: ProgramFC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2023)</a></cite> breaks down complex claims into simpler sub-claims and TOG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024)</a></cite> utilizes a LLM agent iteratively to perform beam search on the KG to discover the most promising reasoning paths. CoRAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khaliq et al. (2024)</a></cite> is a real-time retrieval-based model. Phi3-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al. (2024)</a></cite> is the LLM backbone that we utilize for DYNAMO and the LLM-based baselines. Here we use the form of direct questioning to prompt Phi3-mini to assess the authenticity of the news.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Settings</td>
